INT. VICTORIAN PHOTOGRAPHER'S STUDIO - DAY

1889. A high glass roof throws white light on a painted backdrop of a Roman ruin. A wooden camera on a tripod, its black cloth hanging. Plaster columns, a wicker chair, a potted palm. The room smells of chemicals and dust.

PROFESSOR ALARIC FENWICK, 60s, the photographer, in a velvet smoking jacket and a skullcap, ducks beneath the cloth and adjusts a brass lens. Frail and exacting.

LADY CORDELIA VANE, 50s, in a stiff black silk gown, sits in the wicker chair with hands folded in her lap. Her jaw is a drawn line.

Behind her, CAPTAIN EDMUND VANE, 50s, her husband, stands in dress uniform with medals pinned to his chest. His posture is perfect. His eyes are far away.

FENWICK
(from under the cloth)
A little to the left, Lady Vane.

CORDELIA
I am quite comfortable here.

FENWICK
The light, my lady.

CORDELIA
The light will survive.

FENWICK
Captain Vane, perhaps you might lean forward.

EDMUND
I prefer to stand as I am.

FENWICK
Of course.

He emerges, dusts his hands, and examines them both with the squint of a man arranging a bouquet.

FENWICK (CONT'D)
You are, if I may, rather stiff.

CORDELIA
We are celebrating thirty years, Professor.

FENWICK
Then smile.

CORDELIA
We are not a smiling family.

EDMUND
Cordelia.

CORDELIA
Edmund.

A silence. The camera ticks as the shutter cools. Fenwick pretends to adjust the lens.

FENWICK
I have photographed many couples, my lady. Some embrace, some glare, some stare at the ceiling. The photograph keeps everything.

CORDELIA
Does it?

FENWICK
Every shadow.

EDMUND
Let it keep this one, then.

He lays his hand upon her shoulder. She stiffens. Then, very slightly, leans into it.

FENWICK
Hold that.

CORDELIA
Do not tell us when.

FENWICK
(ducking under the cloth)
I never do.

A bright puff of magnesium. White light floods the studio. When it fades, they have not moved. Edmund's hand is still on her shoulder.

CORDELIA
(softly)
Edmund, you may remove your hand now.

EDMUND
I think I shall not.
